      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Three · Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">The Canon</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">Who Gave the Church the New Testament?</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">The Old Testament came with Yahuah's seal. The New Testament list came from a bishop's letter and a council's vote. The book in the believer's lap was handed down by the system Scripture says to come out of.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">INTRODUCTION</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Who Decided?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">A believer holds a book in his hands and calls it the Word of Yahuah: 66 books, 39 in the Old Testament and 27 in the New. He has been told this so often, from so young an age, that the question never occurs to him: who decided?</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Who looked at the writings circulating in the ancient world — gospels, letters, apocalypses, sermons — and drew the line? Who said, "These twenty-seven are Scripture. The rest are not"? Where is the verse in the Bible that authorizes the men who decided what would be in the Bible?</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The verse does not exist. There is no moment in Scripture where Yahuah authorizes a New Testament, no moment where Yahushua commissions the collecting of one, and no moment where the apostles sign off on a list. The answer goes by names most believers have never heard spoken aloud — Marcion, Athanasius, Carthage, Constantine — and the religion they served is the one believers are told they have escaped.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The canon is not a practice the church does. It is something the church was handed and never checked. Scripture names the power behind what turns believers from the truth: "some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits" (1 Timothy 4:1). Against it stands the Spirit of truth, who "will guide you into all truth" (John 16:13). The Greek word <em>kanōn</em> means a measuring rod, a rule. Scripture's own rod was the Law and the Prophets: "To the law and to the testimony: if they speak not according to this word, it is because there is no light in them" (Isaiah 8:20). <strong>In the canon, the enemy took the measuring rod.</strong> He moved it from the Law and the Testimony to a council's list.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">I</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">A Stamp Without a Match</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The Old Testament has a chain of custody Yahuah Himself authorized. He gave the Torah directly to Mosheh on Sinai. He spoke through named prophets whose words were preserved. The book of the Law was placed beside the Ark (Deuteronomy 31:26). He commanded kings to copy the Law with their own hands (Deuteronomy 17:18). Yahushua confirmed the three divisions of the Hebrew Scriptures when He said, "All things must be fulfilled, which were written in the law of Moses, and in the prophets, and in the psalms, concerning me" (Luke 24:44). That is a stamp. That is a chain of custody.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">There is no matching stamp for the New Testament. There is no command from Yahushua to assemble a second collection of Scripture, and no promise from the Father to inspire one. The writers of the New Testament books did not know they were writing "the New Testament." Paul wrote letters to specific assemblies about specific problems. Luke wrote a historical account to one man named Theophilus. The apostles preached, taught, and discipled, and none of them said "add this to the Book."</p>

      <p class="cod-body">When Paul wrote his final letters to Timothy, he did not say the sacred canon was complete. He told Timothy to continue in the Scriptures (2 Timothy 3:15-16) — the holy Scriptures Timothy had known "from a child," which could only be the Old Testament. The New Testament, as a collection, has no stamp from Yahuah, from Yahushua, or from the apostles. The only stamp it carries is from men who came later, men who had already absorbed the religion of Rome. The Spirit of truth gave the stamp to the Law and the Prophets; the list came from somewhere else.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Did the Apostles Call Each Other Scripture?</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">Two verses are raised against this point. In 1 Timothy 5:18 Paul writes, "the scripture saith," quotes Moses on the ox that treads out the corn (Deuteronomy 25:4), and then adds "The labourer is worthy of his reward" — words Yahushua spoke in Luke 10:7. In 2 Peter 3:16 Peter says the unlearned twist Paul's letters "as they do also the other scriptures."</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Take both at full weight. The apostles honored the Messiah's words and one another's teaching as carrying Yahuah's authority. That is not in dispute, and it is not what this chapter questions. Neither verse names a list, closes a collection, or says which books belong in a second Testament. Peter does not say which of Paul's letters he means, and Paul's "the scripture saith" stands on Moses first.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The question was never whether the apostles spoke truth. The question is who decided the 27-book list, and when. The answer to that is still Athanasius in 367 and the councils that followed him.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">II</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Promised Before It Was Written</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The first objection a believer raises here is the obvious one: without the New Testament, how would anyone know what the new covenant looks like? The objection sounds strong until the Old Testament is opened and the new covenant is found described there in detail, centuries before any apostle wrote a word.</p>

      <blockquote class="cod-scripture">
        <p class="cod-scripture-text">Behold, the days come, saith Yahuah, that I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel … I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts; and will be their God, and they shall be my people … for I will forgive their iniquity, and I will remember their sin no more</p>
        <p class="cod-scripture-ref">— Jeremiah 31:31, 33-34</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="cod-body">The new covenant was announced by Yahuah Himself, through Jeremiah, six hundred years before Yahushua was born. It is made with Israel. Its mechanism is the Law written in the heart. Its purpose is forgiveness of iniquity. Every essential feature is in this passage, long before any apostle existed.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Ezekiel was given the parallel: "A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you … And I will put my spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes" (Ezekiel 36:26-27). A new heart, a new spirit, the pouring out of the Set-Apart Spirit, and the power to walk in the statutes — not the abolition of them. Six hundred years before Acts 2, the Old Testament described the very Shavuot the New Testament would later report. Then Isaiah 53 gives the Suffering Servant who bears the sin of many and makes intercession for the transgressors. The portrait of the Messiah, His work, the new covenant He would bring in, and the Spirit He would pour out are all in the Old Testament before a single New Testament page was written.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">This is why Yahushua never told the apostles to write a new book. He told them to preach the kingdom, to make disciples, and to teach them to observe all things He had commanded (Matthew 28:19-20) — and what He had commanded was the Torah understood through Him (Matthew 5:17-19). The foundation was already laid. He came to fulfill it, not to author a sequel.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The new covenant did not need a New Testament to exist. It already existed in the Old.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body">The New Testament writings themselves quote and confirm the Old Testament. They never replace it. Paul cites Habakkuk to explain justification by belief. Hebrews quotes Jeremiah's new-covenant passage in full. Yahushua quoted Deuteronomy three times to silence the adversary in the wilderness. Every doctrine the apostles teach, they teach by going back to what was already written. The Old Testament does not quote the New, because it does not need to. The New quotes the Old on nearly every page, because that is the foundation it stands on. That is the measuring rod of the Spirit of truth.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">III</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Marcion Forced the Question (A.D. 140-144)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Most believers have never heard the name, yet he is one of the most consequential figures in early church history: Marcion of Sinope.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Marcion was the wealthy son of a bishop, born around A.D. 85 in what is now northern Turkey. He arrived in Rome around A.D. 140, and within a few years he was teaching a system so radical that the Roman church excommunicated him in A.D. 144. Before they threw him out, Marcion did something no one before him had done: he proposed his own canon of Scripture.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">His theology came down to three claims. First, the God of the Old Testament was a different and lower god than the Father of Yahushua — tribal, vengeful, inferior. Second, the Law of Moses was evil and had to be rejected entirely. Third, Yahushua came to free humanity from the God of the Old Testament.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">From those claims he built his canon. He threw out the entire Old Testament. He kept only an edited Luke and ten of Paul's letters, and even those he edited to remove positive references to the Law, to Israel, and to the Hebrew Scriptures. It was the first formal canon list in Christian history.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The first man to draw a line around what counted as Scripture in the Christian movement was a man who hated the Torah, hated the Father of the Old Testament, and built his list to cut Yahushua off from His own people. That is the soil the canonization process grew out of. Rome's answer was to form its own list — partly against him, but within the question he had framed. The conversation he started was the conversation Athanasius and Carthage finished. <strong>When the measuring rod is taken from the Law and handed to a man who hates the Law, a seducing spirit is holding it.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>Marcion was excommunicated. His theology was canonized.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body">The evangelical pulpit today says, "We're under grace, not Law," "The Old Testament was for them; the New Testament is for us," "The God of the Old Testament is harsh; the God of the New Testament is loving," and "Yahushua came to free us from rules." Every one of those statements is Marcion — softer in tone, same content. The man Rome formally rejected became the spiritual ancestor of the doctrine that fills evangelical pews on Sunday morning.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">IV</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Men Who Locked It In (A.D. 110-397)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">By the time the canonization process produced an official 27-book list, the books themselves had been circulating for two and three centuries. Ignatius of Antioch echoed several of them by A.D. 110. The Muratorian Fragment, usually dated around A.D. 170, lists most of them. Irenaeus of Lyon, around A.D. 180, treats most of the 27 as Scripture. The books were not invented in a fourth-century back room. They were in use long before any council voted on them.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">But the official act of canonization — the moment the list was institutionally fixed and other writings institutionally set aside — came much later. The exact 27 books appear together for the first time in the Easter letter of Athanasius, bishop of Alexandria, in A.D. 367, three hundred and thirty years after the resurrection. An Egyptian bishop wrote a pastoral letter to his churches and included a list. That is the moment.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Three decades later, the list was affirmed at the Synod of Hippo (393) and the Council of Carthage (397), both held in North Africa under bishops of the Roman imperial church. The Council of Carthage did not receive a revelation. It received a document, and the bishops signed off.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Athanasius was not a neutral scribe. He was locked in a decades-long fight over the doctrine of the Trinity, a doctrine not found in Scripture in the form he defended it. He was the chief defender of the Nicene "one substance" formula in the decades after the council, exiled and restored several times depending on which emperor favored him. The man who handed Christianity its canon was also the man who hardened the Trinity into orthodoxy. The Council of Carthage was not a gathering of apostles. It was a gathering of bishops already under Roman authority — already baptizing infants, praying to saints, keeping Sunday as the day of worship, and treating the Law as obsolete. The same council that signed off on the New Testament also included books in the Old Testament that most Protestants now reject as apocrypha.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Above all of these men stood Constantine, the emperor whose backing turned the Christian movement into the religion of the Roman state. His Council of Nicaea in 325 hardened the Trinitarian creed under his political pressure, and in 321 he made "the venerable day of the Sun" the empire's day of rest. In the same century, Christmas was fixed on December 25, the day Rome kept for Sol Invictus. The canonization process happened inside the merger of Christianity with the religion of the sun. <strong>A seducing spirit did not need to forge a book. It only needed to choose who held the rod.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Constantine and the Sun-Cult Merger</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">Constantine honored Sol Invictus, the "Unconquered Sun," for much of his reign. Coins minted in those years show Sol Invictus on the reverse alongside his own image. His arch in Rome credits his victory to an unnamed "divinity," with the sun god carved on its face. He was baptized only on his deathbed. The man who presided over the first ecumenical council of Christianity had been a sun worshipper.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">He came to Christianity looking less for truth than for unity. His empire was divided, and several religions competed for the loyalty of his people, including the cult of Sol Invictus, the mysteries of Mithras, and the growing Christian movement. His strategy was not to crush the others but to bring them together under one imperial religion.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Symbols of the sun cult — the halo, the solar disk, the rays around the head — appear increasingly in Christian art from the fourth century on. "The venerable day of the Sun" became the empire's day of rest by imperial decree in 321, and the Council of Laodicea, about forty years later, forbade believers to rest on the Sabbath. December 25 was kept in Rome as the birthday of the Unconquered Sun, and both feasts are first recorded side by side in the Roman calendar of 354. The English name Easter is traced by the early English historian Bede to a spring goddess, Eostre.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">In the middle of this merger, bishops working inside the same imperial system settled which books believers would read. The full Constantine story is covered in Chapter Five, Language and Symbols. For the canon question, the relevant fact is this: the religion that produced the Bible's list was being merged with sun-worship at the same time it was settling the books. The two events cannot be separated.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — The Books They Set Aside</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">The early believers, the ones who lived closest to the apostles, were reading more than the 27 books that made the list.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The Shepherd of Hermas.</strong> Written in the early second century, it was one of the most widely read books in the earliest assemblies. Irenaeus, Clement of Alexandria, and Origen quoted it as Scripture, and it is bound with the New Testament in the Codex Sinaiticus. It calls believers to repentance, holiness, and obedience to the commandments. Athanasius left it out of his list, though in the same letter he recommended it, with the Didache, as reading for new converts.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The Didache — the Teaching of the Twelve.</strong> A first-century manual of discipleship. It opens with the Two Ways — the Way of Life and the Way of Death — and its instructions line up with the Torah. Some of the earliest assemblies treated it as Scripture. It was left out of the list. It is the most Hebraic and law-shaped of the early writings, in a church that was moving away from both.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>1 Clement.</strong> A letter from the assembly at Rome to the assembly at Corinth, around A.D. 95, possibly the earliest Christian writing outside the New Testament. It was read as Scripture in assemblies in Egypt and Syria for centuries and is bound into the Codex Alexandrinus. It was left out. It speaks of the Father in heaven and the Son at His right hand as two, in language far plainer than the Trinitarian formulas the later councils were building.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Jubilees and Enoch.</strong> Jude cites Enoch by name and quotes him as prophecy (Jude 14-15). Jubilees was treated as authoritative by the community at Qumran, and both books are still in the Ethiopian Bible. Both were left out of the Western canon. Jubilees is named here for the record, not as a guide: its own calendar condemns reckoning by the moon (Jubilees 6:36-38), and this site does not follow it.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The pattern is plain. The books that were set aside were, for the most part, books that would have anchored believers to the Torah, to the Hebrew calendar, and to a Father and Son who are two. The books that were kept were the easiest to stretch into the framework a Gentile Roman religion needed. That is curation by a committee.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">V</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Revelation Is Different</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Inside the New Testament itself there is a difference most believers have never been taught to see. Twenty-six of the books are letters and eyewitness accounts. One book is something else.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Matthew opens with a genealogy and a historical account. Mark begins, "The beginning of the gospel of Yahushua Messiah." Luke opens with a dedication to Theophilus, as a careful historian. Paul's epistles are letters: "Paul, an apostle … to the saints which are at Ephesus." The general epistles are letters. Every one of these books fits a known category — eyewitness testimony, ordered narrative, or pastoral letter.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Then the last book opens with a sentence unlike anything before it: "The Revelation of Yahushua Messiah, which God gave unto him, to shew unto his servants things which must shortly come to pass; and he sent and signified it by his angel unto his servant John" (Revelation 1:1).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">This is not "Paul, an apostle." This is the language of the Old Testament prophets: a revelation from Yahuah, given to His Son, sent through a heavenly messenger, and written down by His servant. It is the opening of Ezekiel's vision, Daniel receiving his interpretations, and Zechariah shown the lampstand. Revelation does not claim to be eyewitness history. It claims to be vision-revelation in the same prophetic line as the canonical prophets.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The New Testament reads in two categories. Matthew through Jude are faithful eyewitness testimony, measured against the foundation of the Torah and the Prophets. Revelation belongs in the prophetic category alongside Daniel, Ezekiel, and Zechariah — direct revelation from the throne, read with the same care and caution as the visions of the Old Testament prophets.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VI</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Measuring Rod</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Everything before this has been historical groundwork. This is the principle every believer needs in hand to read his Bible safely.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The test is this. If a doctrine appears only in the New Testament — if it cannot be found, taught, or defended from the Old Testament alone — that doctrine is suspect. The new covenant did not introduce new truths about who Yahuah is, what He requires, or how He saves. It fulfilled and clarified what He had already revealed. Anything that grew out of the New Testament writings without already being present in the Old is not new revelation. It is later interpretation laid over the apostles' eyewitness accounts.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">When Paul preached in Berea, the believers there did not simply nod along. They "searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so" (Acts 17:11) — the Hebrew Scriptures, to test what Paul preached. The apostles' words were measured by the prophets' words. The new was tested by the old. The order of authority was clear: foundation first, witness second. This is the Spirit of truth's measuring rod.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Modern Christianity has reversed it. New Testament verses are quoted to overturn Old Testament commandments. Parables are stretched into doctrines the prophets never described. Then apologists go back to the Old Testament with the new doctrine already in hand and twist its verses to fit, so they can say, "It's in the Old Testament too." <strong>Turning the rod around, so the later writing measures the earlier, is a seducing spirit's work.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body"><strong>The five-step pattern of doctrinal evolution.</strong> Every false doctrine in modern Christianity follows the same shape:</p>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>One.</strong> A doctrine is born after the apostles, often in reaction to a heresy or a political need.</li>
        <li><strong>Two.</strong> Apologists search the New Testament for verses that, read in isolation, can be made to support it.</li>
        <li><strong>Three.</strong> Those verses get translation, punctuation, and emphasis adjustments to strengthen the new reading.</li>
        <li><strong>Four.</strong> Apologists then go back to the Old Testament and find passages that, with creative reinterpretation, can be bent to fit the new doctrine.</li>
        <li><strong>Five.</strong> The doctrine is now declared "biblical" because it has New Testament support (manufactured) and Old Testament support (twisted), and anyone who challenges it is accused of attacking Scripture itself.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="cod-body">This pattern produced the Trinity, the abolished Law, the immortal soul, the Sunday Sabbath, eternal conscious torment, the rapture, infant baptism, the veneration of Mary, and many others. Every one came after the apostles. Every one required Old Testament verses to be pulled from their context. Every one is what this test is designed to catch.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">For any doctrine in the modern church, the question is: can it be defended from the Old Testament alone, without bringing Paul's letters in as the senior witness? If it can, it is consistent with what Yahuah has always revealed. If it cannot — if the doctrine collapses without New Testament proof-texts — it is not biblical theology. It is interpretation pretending to be revelation.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Doctrines That Evolved After the Apostles</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">A short tour of the doctrines that fail the test — each one impossible to defend from the Old Testament alone, each one developed after the apostles, and each one supported by bending both New and Old Testament verses after the fact.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The Trinity.</strong> Three co-equal Persons in one substance, not formalized until the Council of Constantinople in A.D. 381, three hundred and fifty years after the resurrection. The Hebrew Scriptures know one Yahuah and a coming Messiah. The "Trinity verse" most believers cite, 1 John 5:7, is not in any Greek manuscript before the late Middle Ages; it entered through the Latin. It is defended now by turning Genesis 1:26 ("let us make man") and Isaiah 6:8 ("who will go for us") into proof-texts they were never written to be.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The abolished Law.</strong> Marcion's teaching, rejected as heresy in A.D. 144, is now standard evangelical doctrine. It cannot be defended from the Old Testament: every prophet who described the new covenant described the Law being written inward, not abolished (Jeremiah 31:33; Ezekiel 36:27). It is defended only by cutting Paul's letters off from their Hebrew context.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The immortal soul.</strong> From Greek philosophy, Plato in particular, not from Scripture. The Hebrew Scriptures know <em>nephesh</em>, a living being, not an immortal soul. The dead "sleep" until the resurrection. The Greek idea was grafted in by the church fathers and then defended by bending Old Testament passages about Sheol into a heaven-or-hell framework.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Sunday as the Sabbath.</strong> Decreed by Constantine in A.D. 321 and backed by the Council of Laodicea around A.D. 363. It has no Old Testament basis. It is defended now by misreading Acts 20:7 (a single evening meeting) and 1 Corinthians 16:2 (a collection set aside at home) as decrees changing the Sabbath. The Sabbath was a creation ordinance, built into Genesis 2 before any nation existed.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Eternal conscious torment.</strong> Not what the Hebrew <em>Sheol</em> means (the grave). Not what Yahushua's word <em>Gehenna</em> means (a real valley used as a picture of final destruction). Not what Yahuah promises in Malachi 4:1, where the wicked are burned up as stubble, leaving "neither root nor branch." Taught as early as Tertullian, systematized by Augustine, and fully formalized in the Middle Ages.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The rapture.</strong> It did not exist in Christian theology before John Nelson Darby and the Plymouth Brethren in the 1830s, less than two hundred years ago. It cannot be defended from the Old Testament at all; the prophets describe one return of the Messiah in glory, not two.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">In every case the pattern holds. The doctrine appears late. It cannot stand on the Old Testament alone. It is propped up with New Testament proof-texts read in isolation, and then Old Testament verses are bent backward to confirm it.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul said the fight is "against principalities, against powers" (Ephesians 6:12). A principality does not need to forge a single verse. It only needs the believer to measure by the wrong rod. Once the later writings judge the earlier ones, any doctrine can be made "biblical," and the Law Yahuah spoke from Sinai can be set aside by a verse lifted from a letter. The believer still holds the whole book, and has lost the rule that tells him how to read it.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The enemy does not need to take the Bible away. He only needs to turn the measuring rod around.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The Old Testament comes with Yahuah's seal, spoken from Sinai, preserved through the prophets, and confirmed by the Messiah's own words. The New Testament list comes with Marcion's pressure, Athanasius's signature, Carthage's vote, and Constantine's shadow over all of it. Two very different chains of custody.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">This does not make the eyewitness accounts worthless. It makes them accountable. The men who walked with Yahushua wrote true things about what they saw. The letters that made the list contain real apostolic instruction. Revelation is genuine vision-revelation in the prophetic line. None of that is destroyed by seeing where the system that compiled them came from. But none of it overrides the foundation either. The witness is measured against the original, not the other way around.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The new covenant did not need a New Testament to exist; Jeremiah, Ezekiel, and Isaiah described it before any apostle was born. The measuring rod catches every later corruption: if a doctrine cannot be defended from the Old Testament alone, it is interpretation pretending to be revelation. Marcion was excommunicated for teaching the abolished Law in A.D. 144, and the same teaching now fills nearly every evangelical pulpit, with the labels filed off. In the canon, the enemy took the measuring rod.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">One more fingerprint. The English word <em>Bible</em> comes from the Greek <em>biblia</em>, books, which traces back through <em>byblos</em>, papyrus, to the Phoenician city of Byblos on the coast of Lebanon, through which papyrus was traded. Byblos was a center of Baal worship. Its patron was Baalat Gebal, "the Lady of Byblos," worshipped as Astarte, the Phoenician form of Ashtoreth, with Baal beside her — the same Ashtoreth Yahuah told Israel to drive out of the land.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">No one needs to feel guilty for using the word. Most believers have used it all their lives without knowing where it came from. But once it is known, it cannot be unknown. The system that handed down the canon also handed down the name for it, and the name comes from a center of Baal worship. Better names are at hand: the Set-Apart Scriptures, the Word of Yahuah, the Tanakh and the Eyewitness Accounts.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Revelation itself shows the rod in Yahuah's hand. John was "given a reed like unto a rod: and the angel stood, saying, Rise, and measure the temple of God, and the altar, and them that worship therein" (Revelation 11:1). The measuring is His, and the rod He gives is His Word.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>Is the doctrine rooted in the foundation Yahuah laid, or in the curation a council made? The rod is in hand. Use it.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body"><em>See also: The Adventures of Barney and Clyde — a story of two books, one Guide, and the source of authority.</em></p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
